{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Test Fixture.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Test Fixture.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"test fixture test fixture","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Test Fixture.md","fileName":"Test Fixture.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Test Fixture","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 20:37\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Web Application Testing]] [[Test-Driven Development and Unit Test Design]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Web Application Testing","Test-Driven Development and Unit Test Design","aspnetcore3andangular9_3ed.pdf","hands-onsoftwarearchitecturewithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Test Fixture.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Test Fixture.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"test fixture test fixture","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Test Fixture.md","fileName":"Test Fixture.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Test Fixture","heading":"Test Fixture","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A test fixture is the prepared environment and state used to exercise a unit. It includes the subject, its configured dependencies, and any data needed before the action occurs.\n\nIn Angular testing, a component fixture gives the test access to both the component instance and its rendered element. TestBed creates that environment from a testing module configuration.\n\nIn xUnit, fixture lifetimes can share costly initialization across tests or classes, but the fixture must not introduce mutable state that makes cases depend on execution order.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Web Application Testing","Test-Driven Development and Unit Test Design","aspnetcore3andangular9_3ed.pdf","hands-onsoftwarearchitecturewithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Not